<p>Pope Leo XIV used a high-security stop in Bamenda, Cameroon, on Thursday to deliver one of his sharpest denunciations of war, condemning “a handful of tyrants” who spend billions on conflict and warning against leaders who exploit religion and natural resources for military or political ends. The remarks came during his African tour and against the backdrop of a widening public clash with President Trump over the pope’s refusal to support the war in Iran. The visit also drew attention to Cameroon’s Anglophone crisis, as Bamenda has become a powerful symbol of violence in the country’s Northwest Region.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/zqO2bWVC5OUbpUBOV3BWqV_kS18mlCH_h5FWupfq9p25YRiCswK72mxnx2_OUXYvrP_kYAXgKVJYFFxMqKh_xvBCMiCvUg6Tagoy5BMRxWsDblm3A1x2XZB2POOInjsLstBinF0VwJOAcfhEwuQrVeTIyxYOqWS9FRNcEDLCK7Udv3YBJURmzUgPl2efIS15dtZNxKyTBlrpLFCKQt3-dwWG8EapizmjR9jvBJM8J_XRc8ES4oO4ZLxKyFEEBQdtKaEj5NGI0RUC3hqStA' alt='Pope Leo XIV waves from the Popemobile to people gathering as he arrives to lead Holy Mass at Bamenda Airport in Cameroon on Thursday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Solidarity counterpoint: Leo said the world may be ravaged by tyrants but is still held together by “a multitude of supportive brothers and sisters,” shifting attention from elite violence to everyday acts of peace.</li><li>Resource critique: Leo said those who rob Africa of its resources often reinvest profits in weapons, linking extraction to an “endless cycle of destabilisation and death” in one of the sharpest economic critiques of his trip.</li><li>Unnamed rebuke: Although the speech landed amid a feud with President Trump, Leo did not name any country or leader directly, keeping the message universal even as coverage linked it to current wars and diplomacy.</li><li>‘Masters of war’ language: Coverage by The Globe and Mail highlighted Leo’s attack on “masters of war,” broadening his target from individual tyrants to the wider political machinery that sustains conflict.</li></ul><blockquote>Woe to those who manipulate religion and the very name of God for their own military, economic and political gain, dragging that which is sacred into darkness and filth. - Pope Leo XIV</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pope Leo XIV: He framed war as a moral, economic and spiritual failure, condemning leaders who spend vast sums on destruction while neglecting healing, education and restoration. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/pope-leo-excoriates-tyrants-waging-war-instead-of-peace/a-76813305'>DW</a>)</li><li>President Trump: President Trump publicly criticized the pope during their dispute over the Iran war, with BBC reporting he called Leo weak on crime while other outlets said Trump and allies attacked the pontiff for refusing to back the conflict. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvg0z3n5e5jo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Peace movement supporters: The pope explicitly praised peace movements during the Cameroon stop, presenting them as a constructive counterweight to leaders who fuel conflict. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/pope-blasts-tyrants-ravaging-earth-during-cameroon-visit/article70870794.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Archbishop of Canterbury: The Archbishop of Canterbury backed the pontiff after the dispute with President Trump, according to Dawn’s report on reactions around the visit. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992444/handful-of-tyrants-ravaging-world-peace-says-pope'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202604170091.html'>Cameroon: Pope Leo Excoriates &#x27;Tyrants&#x27; Waging War Instead of Peace</a> - allafrica.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/pope-blasts-tyrants-ravaging-earth-during-cameroon-visit/article70870794.ece'>Pope blasts ‘tyrants’ ravaging Earth during Cameroon visit</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/17/pope-leo-xiv-denounces-a-handful-of-tyrants-destroyin